WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.150
Film Courage: Should a new writer look at
writing as a fun endeavor or should they really

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:08.790
check themselves in the beginning and say
"Okay is this something I want to take on,"

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.599
the time commitment, time away from maybe
friends and family, maybe there are going

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:15.640
to be parts that won't be so joyous?

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:22.210
Brooks Elms, Screenwriter/Filmmaker: It's
a great question and yeah absolutely, do it

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.470
because it's fun.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:30.090
There's no other reason to do it and if it's
not completely fun, do anything else.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.470
Find what is fun for you.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.230
Film Courage: If there was something that
was a hobby for you that you loved but you

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.540
didn't want to take it on as a career, could
you say the same for writers maybe?

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.910
They just want to write for the sheer joy
of it, for creating characters but they don't

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:50.090
want the pressure of having to pitch and have
to pay their car payment based on whether

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:51.370
they sell a script or not?

00:00:51.370 --> 00:01:00.059
Brooks: Yeah of course, that's one of the
most satisfying experiences I get from writing.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:07.049
The fact that I get paid well to write is
separate from my experience of writing.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:11.749
I mean if I didn't make a dime from anything
I wrote the rest of my life I would still

00:01:11.749 --> 00:01:23.700
write I mean it's because it's so deeply satisfying
in terms of executing my vision for how life

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.890
occurs to me.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.539
What are my deepest challenges?

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:32.350
What are the insights that have sort of gotten
me through different parts of my life how

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.939
do i how do i make a transformation from this
stage to that stage i mean that's what we

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:43.569
do in any screenplay and if i'm writing about
an astronaut or this or that um you know i've

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:48.399
never been an astronaut but i've written about
several um but it's the emotional journey

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:54.359
of those characters that's it's as personal
to me as if i'm writing in a journal so um

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:59.239
if somebody is drawn to screenwriting for
the sheer joy of it that's absolutely what

00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:03.340
they should be doing and they and nobody really
should be worrying about the money side of

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:12.659
it if it's so fun for you to write a screenplay
and so fun for you to share it with anybody

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:20.790
and so fun for them to say i love it and so
fun for them to sort of give you feedback

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:27.060
and so fun for you to take that feedback and
find a way to serve them even better and so

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:33.939
fun to keep that going then absolutely that
person should allow the professional part

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:38.849
of it to come to them and if they stop at
any point in that path and they're happy just

00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:43.010
writing it for themselves and showing it to
nobody that's great i mean it's really about

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:50.269
what makes them happy is that the litmus test
is it fun yeah really so is that how you approach

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:54.720
most things in life though it has to be where
it's not creating huge amounts of stress for

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:01.989
you yeah um absolutely i read a wonderful
book years ago about ben and jerry's ice cream

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:08.480
company um i love i loved it so much i cried
when i finished uh reading it and it was because

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:15.599
they to my mind had a beautiful vision of
fun and business you know it's show and its

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:21.239
business and the way they articulated it was
this double bottom line anything they did

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:28.230
had had to be fun and it had to be profitable
right um and the truth is um if you approach

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:34.250
it the right way um you absolutely can do
both and if you're not seeing the way to do

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:40.441
both have something be profitable and lucrative
and fun and easy you're not looking in the

00:03:40.441 --> 00:03:44.269
right direction it's probably like right behind
you you got to turn around and look someplace

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:50.400
else but it really is and how in my writing
process or my life certainly things come up

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.641
and it feels there's a resistance or something's
not easy and then that's just a reminder to

00:03:55.641 --> 00:03:59.639
go dude it's you know you're this is you're
forcing it it's too much and then i go okay

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:04.629
well what's the easy what's the easy way what's
the easy way and that's oftentimes it's stopping

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:07.719
and breathing or we're not thinking about
it think about something else that is easy

00:04:07.719 --> 00:04:15.730
in the moment and then going to that um and
then um when i hold those spaces open the

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:22.960
easy ways come have you had people challenge
you students mentees that say work isn't supposed

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.419
to be fun aside from this interview where
i'm playing the devil's advocate i love it

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:38.020
well it's um i mean that's the standard right
so um uh and and and so it's where and and

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:44.940
people are people have to i can sit here and
tell them this or that or the other and uh

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:50.600
they they need to figure it out for themselves
and they need to try it if they're interested

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.699
and not try it if they're not interested it's
not right for them if they hear it it's too

00:04:54.699 --> 00:05:03.540
crazy and you know that's fine so um i have
no interest in um uh convincing somebody of

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.740
anything i mean if i you know when i used
to teach this class at uc ucla extension i

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:14.310
would absolutely speak to these ideas um and
they wouldn't really challenge me i'm sure

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:18.680
some people were very skeptical and some people
were flat out like that's crazy that can't

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.120
work and that's fine you know it certainly
can't work for them if that's the way they

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:30.400
think um and um it might work for you even
if you're skeptical if you try it and you

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.669
say okay i think it's probably not going to
work but i'm going to be open to it and i

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:41.060
might try it and you see and what will happen
at least in my experience is um a it feels

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:47.280
better and if nothing else if the ah if there's
no law of attraction if it's all hui and um

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:53.759
the odds of something happening are exactly
the same but your approach to life feels better

00:05:53.759 --> 00:05:58.720
and you've got the same odds it's worth doing
it so my invitation to anybody who's skeptical

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.190
is i honor the skepticism try it out see if
you enjoy it more see if the odds are at least

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:10.090
the same of getting what you want as soon
as you want and and see if you don't have

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:13.810
the experience that i've had and i've seen
pretty much everybody that embraces the theory

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:21.950
have you actually get things faster because
i'm open to opportunities in a way that's

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:26.530
different when i'm closed off and i'm focused
on oh crap this is going to happen i'm scared

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:31.080
of that or this didn't happen or i'm not good
enough or i'm an imposter or all these things

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.889
these voices that come up um you know it's
good to notice that they're there and just

00:06:36.889 --> 00:06:41.580
let them go and if we spend too much time
on them or we'll spend any time just just

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:47.430
notice them like the wind and let it go through
because the more we sort of give them attention

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:52.069
the more we're sort of limiting ourselves
so you're also not attached to other people's

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:56.979
opinions it sounds like too which is a beautiful
place to be it's the best place to be because

00:06:56.979 --> 00:07:07.580
um my i'm here to serve my audience so um
if i have um you know so so you know i'll

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:13.300
have a bunch of ideas in my script that i've
put down and when i get feedback i'm it's

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:18.090
not me getting feedback it's like these ideas
came to me they're in service to the audience

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:23.139
and my job is to sort of get out of the way
whatever the best idea from anywhere to better

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:30.289
serve the audience and so when i get feedback
and criticism if there's any peace in that

00:07:30.289 --> 00:07:33.819
that's going to be more of service to the
audience awesome and if it's not going to

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:38.300
be in service okay fine thanks for at least
you know trying to give me feedback you know

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.260
so uh does that answer your question it does
yeah.

